# XSH pthread_cond_broadcast() and pthread_cond_signal()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Spurious wakeups are allowed (pthread_cond_wait() DESCRIPTION): every
# wait sits in a loop on its predicate, no test counts wakeups, and a
# signal may wake more than one thread. A missed wakeup is a FAIL only
# after 10 s, since no bound is set on how soon a woken thread runs.

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_signal.001
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_cond_signal() ends the wait of the one thread blocked
           in pthread_cond_wait() or pthread_cond_timedwait(), both when
           the caller holds the mutex and when it has unlocked it first,
           and returns 0.
test       t/001-unblocks.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_signal.002
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With three threads blocked (each waiting for a token), three
           pthread_cond_signal() calls made under one hold of the mutex,
           one token each, end all three waits.
note       While the signaler holds the mutex no woken thread can
           return, so each signal must unblock a thread that is still
           blocked.
test       t/002-one-per-signal.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_signal.003
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       One pthread_cond_broadcast() ends the waits of three blocked
           threads (two in pthread_cond_wait(), one in
           pthread_cond_timedwait()) and returns 0.
test       t/003-broadcast-all.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_signal.004
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_signal.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       Each of three threads unblocked by one pthread_cond_broadcast()
           returns 0 owning the (error-checking) mutex: no two hold it at
           once and each one's pthread_mutex_unlock() succeeds.
test       t/004-broadcast-owns.c
